# Fork Workflow

Fork of [chatwoot/chatwoot](https://github.com/chatwoot/chatwoot).

## Branches (policy)

| Branch | GitHub default | Purpose |
|--------|----------------|---------|
| **`main`** | **Yes** | All fork-specific work — commits, PRs, deploys, feature branches |
| `develop` | No | Mirror of `upstream/develop` — sync only, **never** fork commits |

- **`main`** is the repository default branch on GitHub (`origin/HEAD` → `origin/main`).
- **`origin/develop`** exists only to mirror `upstream/develop`; do not branch from it for fork features, open PRs into it, or push custom changes. A local `develop` may lag until the next safe sync.
- Upstream README still describes upstream git-flow (`develop` base) — **ignore that for this fork**; follow this rule instead.
- **Never push to `upstream` or open a PR against the original project.** Verify `origin` points to `cesar-carlos/chatwoot` before publication.

```bash
# Fork work starts at the current remote main, not an assumed-current local main.
git fetch origin main
git worktree add -b feat/my-feature .codex/worktrees/my-feature origin/main
# Integrate or open a PR only against the fork's main.
```

## Sync & automation

Run `bin/fork-sync-upstream` from a clean worktree when upstream changes need integration. It verifies the fork/original remotes, fetches both, fast-forwards the `develop` mirror only when histories permit, and creates a review worktree/branch from the latest `origin/main`. It **does not reset or rebase `main`**. If histories diverge, stop for manual review; never force-push the published `main`.

In the review worktree, merge `upstream/develop`, resolve conflicts, run relevant tests and `bin/fork-inventory`, then integrate only into the fork's `main`. Do not use `git reset --hard` or an automatic rebase on a published branch to resolve sync conflicts. Preserve unrelated dirty worktrees.

`bin/fork-inventory` records sorted `FORK:` locations in `doc/fork-divergences.txt` without a changing timestamp. This generated file is Git-ignored: save a copy before merging, rerun the script afterward, and compare the two files with `diff -u`. Review marker identities and behavior, not just the count.

Git hooks use **Husky**: `pnpm install` (or `pnpm exec husky install`) activates `.husky/pre-commit` and `.husky/pre-push`. The pre-commit hook blocks commits on `develop`; the pre-push hook checks destination remote/ref, permits only the fork `origin`, and accepts `develop` only as an exact fast-forward mirror of `upstream/develop`. Do **not** set `core.hooksPath` to `.githooks`, which disables the pre-push hook. Hooks are defense in depth; keep GitHub branch protection enabled.

## Change priority (best → worst merge safety)

| # | Approach | When |
|---|----------|------|
| 1 | Data/config only | `InstallationConfig`, feature flags, Rails console |
| 2 | **`custom/` new files** | Services, models, controllers, jobs, builders |
| 3 | **`prepend_mod_with`** | Override OSS/Enterprise without editing upstream body |
| 4 | One-line `# FORK:` hook | Call `custom/` from upstream entry point (routes, scheduler) |
| 5 | Direct upstream edit | Last resort — every changed line marked `# FORK:` / `// FORK:` |

Enterprise SaaS behavior → `enterprise/`; fork features → `custom/`.

## Custom overlay (`custom/`)

Autoloaded via `config/application.rb` (`custom/lib`, `custom/app/**`). `ChatwootApp.extensions` → `%w[enterprise custom]` for `prepend_mod_with`.

```
custom/app/{models/custom,services/custom,controllers,jobs,builders,policies,listeners}/
spec/custom/   # mirror layout; run with bundle exec rspec spec/custom/…
```

OSS classes already call `SomeModel.prepend_mod_with('SomeModel')` — add `Custom::SomeModel` under `custom/app/models/custom/` with no upstream edit. Code in `custom/` usually needs no `# FORK:` markers.

When upstream has no hook, add the smallest FORK entry point (one route, one scheduler line). Never copy entire upstream files.

## Extending Enterprise features (fork)

- Fork business logic that extends Enterprise behavior → full override in `Custom::X` via `prepend_mod_with` (Custom is last in `ChatwootApp.extensions`, so it wins).
- Do **not** add stubs or `# FORK:` hooks inside `enterprise/` only to “open a door” for the fork.
- Last resort: Enterprise model constants/validations that cannot be prepended cleanly (e.g. frozen `PERMISSIONS` arrays) — one marked `# FORK:` line.

## Specs for overlays

- New behavior under `custom/app/...` → mirror focused coverage under `spec/custom/...` in the **same change**.
- Specs in `spec/enterprise/` may exercise the full stack; they do **not** replace Custom overlay coverage.

## FORK markers & upstream edits

```ruby
def limits
  # FORK: self-hosted enterprise unlimited usage
  return fork_unlimited_limits if ChatwootApp.self_hosted_enterprise?
  # …original logic below unchanged…
end
```

- Guards at method **top**; FORK blocks contiguous and self-contained
- Do not reformat, reorder imports, or refactor upstream "while you're there"
- Avoid high-churn files: `routes.rb`, `Gemfile`, `package.json`, large finders/callbacks, shared initializers
- Frontend: new composables/components; one `// FORK:` import/call in upstream when unavoidable

Discover: `bin/fork-inventory` (searches source and configuration formats, including ERB/YAML and workflows).

## Merge & conflicts

Before integration: save/review `doc/fork-divergences.txt` and the upstream diff. Optional read-only dry-run: `git merge-tree $(git merge-base HEAD upstream/develop) HEAD upstream/develop`.

On conflict: inspect both sides, preserve intended fork behavior and all `FORK:` blocks, and compare marker identities and behavior after resolution. An equal marker count alone does not prove a safe merge.

## Anti-patterns

- Upstream edits without `FORK:` markers · duplicating entire upstream classes · renaming upstream symbols
- Interleaving fork and upstream logic · formatting-only diffs in shared files · large fork changes without syncing first

Commit scope for fork work: `feat(fork): …` or `fix(fork): …`. One logical change per commit.
